 ### [Radon Transform](11306-radon-transform.md)
-The Radon transform maps a 2D image to its projections (sinogram) by integrating pixel intensities along parallel lines at various angles [G]. It solves the forward acquisition model in computed tomography, converting spatial domain data into projection domain data [G]. Unlike its inverse counterpart (e.g., Filtered Back-Projection), which reconstructs images from projections, the forward Radon transform generates the measurements required for reconstruction [G]. It is distinguished from other projection methods by its strict parallel-beam geometry and linear integration nature [G].
+The Radon transform maps a 2D image to its projections (sinogram) by integrating pixel intensities along parallel lines at various angles. It solves the forward acquisition model in computed tomography, converting spatial domain data into projection domain data. Unlike its inverse counterpart (e.g., Filtered Back-Projection), which reconstructs images from projections, the forward Radon transform generates the measurements required for reconstruction. It is distinguished from other projection methods by its strict parallel-beam geometry and linear integration nature.
 
 ### [Fan-Beam Radon Transform](11269-fan-beam-radon-transform.md)
-The Fan-Beam Radon Transform computes projections for a geometry where X-rays originate from a single fixed point source and are captured by a linear detector array [G]. Unlike the [Radon Transform](11306-radon-transform.md) which assumes parallel rays, this method models the divergent ray paths inherent to fan-beam acquisition, allowing for direct reconstruction or rebinning to parallel geometry [G]. It is distinguished from parallel-beam by the diverging ray paths [G].
